  "resources": {
   "title": "Your fix isn't showing up? Check the port",
   "summary": "If Vite's default port is taken (often by a dev server you forgot to stop), Vite starts on the next free port. Your fix lives there, while the old tab still shows the old app.",
   "main_link": "https://vite.dev/config/server-options",
   "links": [
    {
     "label": "Vite docs: server.port and server.strictPort",
     "url": "https://vite.dev/config/server-options"
    },
    {
     "label": "Vite source: the port-in-use message",
     "url": "https://github.com/vitejs/vite/blob/main/packages/vite/src/node/http.ts"
    },
    {
     "label": "Vite docs: configuring Vite",
     "url": "https://vite.dev/config/"
    }
   ],
   "steps": [
    {
     "title": "Compare the URL with the server log",
     "text": "Vite's default port is 5173. If that port is already being used, Vite automatically tries the next available one, so the server may not end up on the port you expect. Read the Local: line in the terminal and compare it with the tab you're refreshing."
    },
    {
     "title": "Spot the message",
     "text": "When it has to move, Vite logs this line before starting:",
     "code": "Port 5173 is in use, trying another one..."
    },
    {
     "title": "Stop the old dev server",
     "text": "Something is still holding the old port. Usually it's a dev server you started earlier in another terminal and never stopped. Stop it, and the new one can take the port back next time."
    },
    {
     "title": "Make Vite fail loudly instead",
     "text": "With strictPort set to true, Vite exits if the port is already in use instead of trying the next one.",
     "code": "// vite.config.js\nimport { defineConfig } from 'vite'\n\nexport default defineConfig({\n  server: { strictPort: true },\n})"
    }
   ],
   "notes": [
    "Other dev servers have their own rules: check your framework's docs for what it does when the port is taken."
   ]
  }
 },
